        "content": "# Technical Guide: Adding a New Benchmark to an Optimization Repository\n\nThis guide outlines the standard procedure for integrating a new benchmark into an existing optimization repository. Adhering to these conventions ensures reproducibility, comparability, and long-term maintainability.\n\n## 1. Directory Structure & File Placement\n\nAll new benchmarks must follow the established directory hierarchy. Assume the repo root is `./`.\n\n### A. Scripts (`/benchmarks/<benchmark_name>/scripts/`)\n- **Purpose**: Contains executable code to run the benchmark.\n- **Naming Convention**:\n  - `run.sh` or `run.py`: Main entry point.\n  - `setup.sh`: Environment setup (if not handled by global CI).\n  - `postprocess.py`: Parses raw logs into structured metrics.\n- **Requirements**:\n  - Must accept command-line arguments for configuration (e.g., `--config`, `--output-dir`).\n  - Must be idempotent (safe to re-run).\n  - Must exit with non-zero status on failure.\n\n### B. Data (`/benchmarks/<benchmark_name>/data/`)\n- **Purpose**: Stores input datasets, configurations, and metadata.\n- **Guidelines**:\n  - **Small files** (<10MB): Commit directly to Git.\n  - **Large files** (>10MB): Use Git LFS or reference an external data registry (e.g., S3, HuggingFace) via a `data_manifest.json`.\n  - **Configurations**: Store YAML/JSON configs in `configs/` subdirectory.\n  - **Checksums**: Include `checksums.txt` for all data files to verify integrity.\n\n### C. Notes & Documentation (`/benchmarks/<benchmark_name>/docs/`)\n- **Purpose**: Human-readable context.\n- **Required Files**:\n  - `README.md`: Overview, hardware requirements, expected runtime, and known issues.\n  - `CHANGELOG.md`: Version history of the benchmark itself.\n  - `KNOWN_ISSUES.md`: List of flaky tests or environment-specific quirks.\n\n### D. Patches (`/benchmarks/<benchmark_name>/patches/`)\n- **Purpose**: Store diffs required to adapt upstream code for benchmarking.\n- **Guidelines**:\n  - Name patches descriptively: `fix-memory-leak-v1.2.patch`.\n  - Include a header comment in each patch explaining *why* it’s needed"
